# Baking powder or baking soda: water, acid and heat are different triggers
#
# The two white powders are not interchangeable. This simulation compares
# equal portions while changing one condition at a time. Real baking powder
# contains a mixture of acids whose staged action varies by brand; the named
# material here is a reviewed teaching surrogate, not a recipe for food.
register lv1

# Baking soda in cold water dissolves. The aqueous equilibrium can release a
# small amount of CO2 even without a bottle of acid; that is not the same route
# as heat-driven decomposition.
add v1 water 50mL
add v1 baking_soda 5g
inspect v1

# The named baking-powder surrogate carries bicarbonate and starch. Warming it
# drives another modeled release of CO2. It does not reproduce the staged acid
# systems used by particular commercial double-acting powders.
new beaker
add v2 water 50mL
add v2 baking_powder 5g
measure v2 thermometer
heat v2 10kJ
measure v2 thermometer
inspect v2

# Acid is a different route to the same visible gas. This control uses ordinary
# 5% vinegar. The bench does not model cake crumb, dough elasticity, oven
# moisture or whether a baked food has risen.
new beaker
add v3 white_vinegar_5_percent 50mL
add v3 baking_soda 5g
inspect v3
